# Lab 01: summarization against Ollama (llama3.2).
# Three requests: the naive prompt (step 1), the improved prompt on the same
# report (step 2), and the improved prompt on the report with the buried
# hazard (step 3). Report text is inlined so these run as-is.
#
# The prompts here are byte-identical to the ones in
# ../dotnet/complete/Program.cs. Keep them that way: llama3.2 is small enough
# that whitespace changes alone move its behavior.

### 1. Naive prompt, report tr-0001 (expect a faithful, useless book report)
POST http://localhost:11434/api/chat
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "model": "llama3.2",
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Summarize this trip report.\n\n# Avalanche Lake, finally, and a full gear debrief\n\nBase weight matters more than people admit, and this trip proved it. I did Avalanche Lake Trail on July 12th with a completely reworked kit and I want to get all of it down while it's fresh, because half the value of a hike like this is the shakedown.\n\nFirst, the trail itself, for those who skim. You pick it up off Trail of the Cedars, that boardwalk loop through the big old-growth stuff, and from there it's something like two point three miles each way to the lake \u2014 call it four and a half, maybe a hair more, round trip. Elevation gain is modest, in the neighborhood of seven hundred feet, spread out gently enough that I never dropped below a three-mile-an-hour pace even with photo stops. Early on you cross the footbridge over Avalanche Creek where it churns through that narrow gorge, and it is worth stopping there even if the crowds bunch up. The water is that milky glacial turquoise that no phone camera gets right.\n\nNow the gear, which is why you're really here.\n\nPack: I finally retired the old 65-liter monster for a 24-liter frameless day pack, 14 ounces empty. For a half-day walk like this, carrying a framed pack is like driving a moving truck to the grocery store. The frameless rode beautifully. One complaint: the side pockets are cut too tight to reach a bottle without a shoulder dislocation.\n\nFootwear: trail runners, not boots. I know, I know, ankle support, blah blah. The tread was overkill honestly \u2014 the trail was in fine shape, packed dirt and roots, a couple of muddy patches maybe a mile in where a seep crosses the tread, nothing a normal human can't step around. Boots would have been four extra pounds of regret.\n\nWater: I carried a liter and a 3-ounce squeeze filter and refilled at the lake inlet. Filtered cold glacial water on a warm July afternoon is one of life's genuinely free luxuries. People hauling three liters from the trailhead for a five-mile day, I salute your training regimen.\n\nLayers: started at 52 degrees at the trailhead around 8am, wind shirt on, wind shirt off within twenty minutes. At the lake there was a breeze coming off the water and the snowfields on the headwall, and the wind shirt earned its 2.1 ounces all over again. There were still white streaks of snow way up on the cliffs above the lake feeding the waterfalls, which in mid-July tells you what kind of snow year it was.\n\nWildlife, since my wife always asks: one mule deer browsing completely unbothered about fifty feet off the trail near the halfway point, and a whole comedy troupe of ground squirrels at the lakeshore who have clearly been fed by tourists and have lost all fear and most of their dignity. One made a serious run at my hip belt pocket. No bears, though a ranger at the trailhead said there'd been a black bear crossing the road near Lake McDonald earlier in the week. Bear spray stayed on the belt, where it should be \u2014 that's one item I will never count grams on.\n\nCrowds: heavy by 10am. If you want the lake to yourself, be walking by 7:30. On my way out around 11 the inbound traffic was a conga line, strollers and flip-flops and one gentleman carrying a full-size bluetooth speaker, may his batteries die swiftly.\n\nVerdict on the kit: total pack weight with water was just under six pounds and I forgot I was wearing it, which is the whole point. Verdict on the trail: an embarrassment of scenery for the effort. Ten waterfalls threading down a two-thousand-foot cirque wall into a lake the color of antifreeze, and all it asks of you is a morning. I'll be back with the tripod and a longer lens, and possibly with the side pockets of that pack re-sewn."
    }
  ],
  "stream": false
}

### 2. Improved prompt, same report (expect 3 hiker-focused bullets, no invented closure)
POST http://localhost:11434/api/chat
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "model": "llama3.2",
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "You are helping a hiker planning to hike this trail within the next week.\nFrom the trip report below, produce exactly 3 bullets covering:\ncurrent trail conditions, hazards or closures, and crowding.\nIgnore gear talk, personal stories, and scenery.\nReport only what the trip report states. Do not turn a wildlife sighting into a\nhazard or a closure, and write \"no closures or hazards reported\" when it says none.\nIf the report does state a closure or hazard, it must appear in the first bullet.\n\n# Avalanche Lake, finally, and a full gear debrief\n\nBase weight matters more than people admit, and this trip proved it. I did Avalanche Lake Trail on July 12th with a completely reworked kit and I want to get all of it down while it's fresh, because half the value of a hike like this is the shakedown.\n\nFirst, the trail itself, for those who skim. You pick it up off Trail of the Cedars, that boardwalk loop through the big old-growth stuff, and from there it's something like two point three miles each way to the lake \u2014 call it four and a half, maybe a hair more, round trip. Elevation gain is modest, in the neighborhood of seven hundred feet, spread out gently enough that I never dropped below a three-mile-an-hour pace even with photo stops. Early on you cross the footbridge over Avalanche Creek where it churns through that narrow gorge, and it is worth stopping there even if the crowds bunch up. The water is that milky glacial turquoise that no phone camera gets right.\n\nNow the gear, which is why you're really here.\n\nPack: I finally retired the old 65-liter monster for a 24-liter frameless day pack, 14 ounces empty. For a half-day walk like this, carrying a framed pack is like driving a moving truck to the grocery store. The frameless rode beautifully. One complaint: the side pockets are cut too tight to reach a bottle without a shoulder dislocation.\n\nFootwear: trail runners, not boots. I know, I know, ankle support, blah blah. The tread was overkill honestly \u2014 the trail was in fine shape, packed dirt and roots, a couple of muddy patches maybe a mile in where a seep crosses the tread, nothing a normal human can't step around. Boots would have been four extra pounds of regret.\n\nWater: I carried a liter and a 3-ounce squeeze filter and refilled at the lake inlet. Filtered cold glacial water on a warm July afternoon is one of life's genuinely free luxuries. People hauling three liters from the trailhead for a five-mile day, I salute your training regimen.\n\nLayers: started at 52 degrees at the trailhead around 8am, wind shirt on, wind shirt off within twenty minutes. At the lake there was a breeze coming off the water and the snowfields on the headwall, and the wind shirt earned its 2.1 ounces all over again. There were still white streaks of snow way up on the cliffs above the lake feeding the waterfalls, which in mid-July tells you what kind of snow year it was.\n\nWildlife, since my wife always asks: one mule deer browsing completely unbothered about fifty feet off the trail near the halfway point, and a whole comedy troupe of ground squirrels at the lakeshore who have clearly been fed by tourists and have lost all fear and most of their dignity. One made a serious run at my hip belt pocket. No bears, though a ranger at the trailhead said there'd been a black bear crossing the road near Lake McDonald earlier in the week. Bear spray stayed on the belt, where it should be \u2014 that's one item I will never count grams on.\n\nCrowds: heavy by 10am. If you want the lake to yourself, be walking by 7:30. On my way out around 11 the inbound traffic was a conga line, strollers and flip-flops and one gentleman carrying a full-size bluetooth speaker, may his batteries die swiftly.\n\nVerdict on the kit: total pack weight with water was just under six pounds and I forgot I was wearing it, which is the whole point. Verdict on the trail: an embarrassment of scenery for the effort. Ten waterfalls threading down a two-thousand-foot cirque wall into a lake the color of antifreeze, and all it asks of you is a morning. I'll be back with the tripod and a longer lens, and possibly with the side pockets of that pack re-sewn."
    }
  ],
  "stream": false
}

### 3. Improved prompt, report tr-0004 (success check: the washed-out bridge surfaces first)
POST http://localhost:11434/api/chat
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "model": "llama3.2",
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "You are helping a hiker planning to hike this trail within the next week.\nFrom the trip report below, produce exactly 3 bullets covering:\ncurrent trail conditions, hazards or closures, and crowding.\nIgnore gear talk, personal stories, and scenery.\nReport only what the trip report states. Do not turn a wildlife sighting into a\nhazard or a closure, and write \"no closures or hazards reported\" when it says none.\nIf the report does state a closure or hazard, it must appear in the first bullet.\n\nForty years ago my father took me to Glacier in a station wagon with fake wood paneling, and I have been trying to get back ever since, so when my sister Diane suggested we finally do it for our combined birthdays, I said yes before she finished the sentence.\n\nWe flew into Kalispell on the 19th of June, and I'll tell you, air travel is not what it was. Our connection through Denver was delayed two hours because of a crew scheduling issue, and the gate agent, bless her, was doing the work of four people. Diane got us sandwiches from that place near gate B22, turkey and some kind of pesto situation, and we ate them sitting on the floor by the window like a couple of teenagers, which honestly set the tone for the whole trip \u2014 we decided right then that nothing was going to bother us this week. The rental car was an upgrade because they'd run out of the compacts, so we ended up in an enormous white SUV that Diane immediately named Moby.\n\nThe plan for Sunday the 21st was Avalanche Lake, which everyone from the campground host to a woman in the huckleberry shop had told us was the hike to do if we only did one. We got to the trailhead area early because parking there is apparently a competitive sport, and started off through Trail of the Cedars, which is this lovely boardwalk under trees so big and old they made us both go quiet. Diane kept touching the bark of the cedars like she was greeting them. The air under there smells like the inside of a hope chest, if that makes sense, cool and sweet and a little like church.\n\nNow here is where I have to be honest about how far we got, and about the morning generally. Maybe a half mile past where the lake trail splits off from the cedar boardwalk, there was a ranger standing at a barricade, and the news was that the footbridge over the creek at the gorge washed out a few days back \u2014 she said high water took it out around the 18th \u2014 and the trail to the lake is closed until they can get a crew in, no reopening date, could be weeks. So no lake for us. The gorge itself, the part you can still see from the barricade area, was absolutely roaring, water the color of a robin's egg smashing through that slot in the rock, and looking at it, neither of us doubted for one second that it could eat a bridge. A young couple next to us was very upset about their plans, and Diane told them what we'd decided at the Denver airport, the thing about not letting anything bother us, and the girl laughed and seemed to feel better.\n\nSo we walked the cedar loop twice, slowly, which I suspect is more than most people give it, and I'm not sure we got the lesser hike, if I'm honest. The second time around we saw a deer, a doe, standing in the ferns not thirty feet away, and she looked at us the way deer do, like they know something.\n\nThat afternoon we drove up the Going-to-the-Sun Road as far as the plowing allowed and pulled over about eleven times for photos. Diane has a rule that you have to actually get out of the car, no photos through the windshield, and she's right, she's always been right about that. We had huckleberry ice cream in Apgar and watched a wedding party taking pictures on the shore of Lake McDonald, the bride holding her shoes in one hand.\n\nMonday we're thinking of trying the Hidden Meadow area or maybe just repeating the cedars a third time, why not, and Tuesday we fly home through Denver again, pray for us. Dad would have loved Moby, is the thing I keep thinking. He would have loved every ridiculous cup holder. Forty years is too long to wait to come back to a place like this, and I'm writing that sentence down where I'll see it."
    }
  ],
  "stream": false
}
